1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,000
The Root of All Evil.

2
00:00:06,001 --> 00:00:09,000
The Stuff Dreams Are Made Of.

3
00:00:09,001 --> 00:00:11,000
Money.

4
00:00:11,001 --> 00:00:17,000
By the Billions Coins and Dollars Flow from Secretive Government Facilities.

5
00:00:17,001 --> 00:00:22,000
Constantly Changing Their Look to Defeat Sophisticated Counterfeiters.

6
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:26,000
Their Storied Past Is As Fantastic As The Fortunes They Make.

7
00:00:26,001 --> 00:00:30,460
Now, U.S. Mints the Money Machines on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:43,000 --> 00:00:47,660
The physical act of making money in the United States is big business.

9
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:54,640
Many of the techniques used by the modern-day money makers were actually developed centuries ago.

10
00:00:56,000 --> 00:00:59,560
when making money was a confusing and often frustrating experience.

11
00:01:02,940 --> 00:01:06,580
The U.S. Bureau of Engraving and Printing, which prints paper money,

12
00:01:06,581 --> 00:01:09,140
and the U.S. Mint, which makes coins,

13
00:01:09,640 --> 00:01:12,640
are some of the busiest manufacturing plants in the world.

14
00:01:15,860 --> 00:01:19,640
The Mint's two production facilities and the Bureau's two printing plants

15
00:01:19,641 --> 00:01:23,020
operate 24 hours a day, 7 days a week,

16
00:01:23,640 --> 00:01:25,420
turning out coin and currency.

17
00:01:26,000 --> 00:01:31,340
The Bureau of Engraving and Printing produces approximately 40 million notes a day.

18
00:01:31,341 --> 00:01:36,220
These notes have a face value of between $600 and $700 million,

19
00:01:36,840 --> 00:01:38,580
depending on the denominations that we're running.

20
00:01:41,140 --> 00:01:46,120
At the U.S. Mint, officials oversee the production of multiple fortunes.

21
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:49,700
We produce up to 20 billion coins a year,

22
00:01:49,701 --> 00:02:00,140
and we sell another $400 million worth of gold, silver, and platinum bullion coins and collector coins that we sell all over the world.

23
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:08,480
Plus, we protect about $100 billion worth of assets of the federal government at Fort Knox and at our other facilities around the country.

24
00:02:11,420 --> 00:02:13,980
Working in conjunction with the Department of the Treasury,

25
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:19,140
the Bureau and Mint continually feed the economy's voracious appetite for new money.

26
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:26,460
Though coins have been around since before Christ, it was the Roman Empire that first

27
00:02:26,461 --> 00:02:32,800
struck coins as a standardized method of currency.

28
00:02:32,801 --> 00:02:39,100
The precious pieces were emblazoned with images of leaders, like Julius Caesar.

29
00:02:39,101 --> 00:02:42,540
The term to strike a coin is still used today.

30
00:02:42,541 --> 00:02:46,080
It comes from the early method used to make a coin.

31
00:02:46,081 --> 00:02:55,020
Artisans would hand-carve metal dies, place the molds over pieces of heated metal, and strike down with a hammer to embed the image onto the new coin.

32
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:07,700
But it was during the tense days of the American Revolution that the world's most powerful form of money, the American dollar, was forged.

33
00:03:09,160 --> 00:03:12,640
There were dozens of forms of money used in the colonies at the time.

34
00:03:12,641 --> 00:03:15,640
Some were from other countries, some from banks,

35
00:03:15,641 --> 00:03:17,640
some from the colonial governments.

36
00:03:17,641 --> 00:03:21,640
The situation that Jefferson viewed

37
00:03:21,641 --> 00:03:24,640
when he advocated this coinage system,

38
00:03:24,641 --> 00:03:28,640
it was a nation in which the United States was a nation

39
00:03:28,641 --> 00:03:31,640
in which we depended upon other nations' coinage.

40
00:03:31,641 --> 00:03:33,640
We used other nations' coinage, and there was probably

41
00:03:33,641 --> 00:03:35,640
four or five different countries' coinage

42
00:03:35,641 --> 00:03:38,640
that circulated as legal tender in the United States.

43
00:03:38,641 --> 00:03:47,480
And it was a mess because every colony had its own exchange rate, and it had multiple forms of coinage that were circulating.

44
00:03:49,540 --> 00:03:55,980
Thomas Jefferson believed the only way the new nation could survive was to have a unified monetary system.

45
00:03:58,120 --> 00:04:02,280
With his backing, Congress enacted the Mint Act of 1792.

46
00:04:02,281 --> 00:04:09,280
The legislation established the nation's first mint in Philadelphia, then the nation's largest city and its capital.

47
00:04:13,280 --> 00:04:18,280
Jefferson appointed the first director of the mint, a scientist named David Rittenhouse.

48
00:04:19,280 --> 00:04:23,280
Many experts believe that in an effort to get the mint into production,

49
00:04:24,280 --> 00:04:29,280
Martha Washington donated her family's silverware for the nation's first silver coins.

50
00:04:32,280 --> 00:04:35,020
And though the government had high expectations for its new mint,

51
00:04:35,420 --> 00:04:37,740
the building itself had more humble beginnings.

52
00:04:38,920 --> 00:04:42,380
They chose an old distillery, an abandoned distillery,

53
00:04:42,920 --> 00:04:47,380
and unfortunately that building has been lost to history.

54
00:04:47,700 --> 00:04:51,980
Ye Old Mint, as it was called, was a pretty modest facility.

55
00:04:51,981 --> 00:04:55,720
It was hand-turned presses,

56
00:04:56,320 --> 00:05:02,020
which a number of men would hand-turn by pressing their bodies against it.

57
00:05:02,280 --> 00:05:09,840
As you can imagine, through a process like that, the production of coinage was slow, and the standards were inconsistent.

58
00:05:13,080 --> 00:05:17,420
In order for new coins to succeed, all other coins would have to be made worthless.

59
00:05:18,280 --> 00:05:19,640
It wasn't a popular idea.

60
00:05:20,280 --> 00:05:26,820
Jefferson really came to understand how over the long term the country just could not conduct its business,

61
00:05:27,000 --> 00:05:29,640
and it could not conduct its economy with this kind of a system.

62
00:05:29,641 --> 00:05:43,040
But it was a tough political sell to convince Congress that they should bite the bullet and spend these huge sums of money to create a mint and create a coinage system.

63
00:05:43,300 --> 00:05:51,620
And the really tough part was to demonetize all that coinage of other nations that was circulating here in the United States.

64
00:05:51,700 --> 00:05:52,680
That was a very tough sell.

65
00:05:53,900 --> 00:05:57,060
Slowly, new U.S. coins began circulating.

66
00:05:57,061 --> 00:06:05,200
gold dollar coins, a half dollar coin, silver dollars, and a half dime, which eventually became the nickel.

67
00:06:08,800 --> 00:06:13,440
There were also two copper coins made, the penny and the half penny.

68
00:06:14,600 --> 00:06:17,920
Designs on the coins were chosen to represent the symbols of the new nation,

69
00:06:19,340 --> 00:06:21,560
Lady Liberty and the American Eagle.

70
00:06:21,561 --> 00:06:27,240
American coins at the time had a value equal to the amount of metal that was in them.

71
00:06:28,940 --> 00:06:34,740
But since the coins were scarce, the colonies, banks, businesses, and even private individuals

72
00:06:34,741 --> 00:06:38,080
took it upon themselves to print early forms of paper money.

73
00:06:40,940 --> 00:06:44,740
Because the paper money was backed solely by a promise by the issuer to pay,

74
00:06:45,380 --> 00:06:47,740
the value of a paper note was always changing.

75
00:06:47,741 --> 00:06:51,900
These bills were early forms of promissory notes.

76
00:06:51,901 --> 00:06:59,480
They would go based on the faith of the people in that government that was issuing the currency,

77
00:06:59,600 --> 00:07:01,360
or the person that was issuing the currency.

78
00:07:01,940 --> 00:07:04,980
Sometimes individual people would issue the currency,

79
00:07:05,420 --> 00:07:11,000
and people would accept the currency because they knew that this person was a man of means

80
00:07:11,001 --> 00:07:14,280
and would take it back, and they could circulate it around,

81
00:07:14,281 --> 00:07:18,100
so they wouldn't have to carry around a bag of gold with them.

82
00:07:20,300 --> 00:07:22,620
There was no formal method of exchange.

83
00:07:23,120 --> 00:07:25,660
What a person could get for their money was up to them.

84
00:07:26,020 --> 00:07:30,500
We got into sort of a tower of babble in paper currency.

85
00:07:31,120 --> 00:07:37,260
We had a similar situation to what Jefferson had sought to alleviate with coinage.

86
00:07:37,261 --> 00:07:44,260
Currency traded around the issuing banks at face value or something close to face value,

87
00:07:44,261 --> 00:07:48,260
but then the further you got away from those issuing banks,

88
00:07:48,261 --> 00:07:55,260
they traded at a discount to face value because of the inconvenience of having to go take them back to that bank.

89
00:07:55,261 --> 00:08:00,260
And then once you got a certain distance away, they weren't worth anything.

90
00:08:00,261 --> 00:08:05,260
No one would accept them because they didn't know what the creditworthiness of that bank was.

91
00:08:07,260 --> 00:08:13,840
The American monetary system became more confusing when the nation experienced its first gold rush in the 1830s.

92
00:08:16,460 --> 00:08:19,920
Gold had been discovered in the mountains of the Carolinas and Georgia.

93
00:08:21,060 --> 00:08:25,100
Mining companies needed a way to get their newfound gold dust into circulation.

94
00:08:25,600 --> 00:08:32,220
The only alternative was to take this gold dust and either ship it to the only mint in the country,

95
00:08:32,340 --> 00:08:36,500
which was in Philadelphia, and the roads were difficult to get to and took a long time,

96
00:08:36,501 --> 00:08:38,440
or to actually use the gold dust.

97
00:08:39,620 --> 00:08:43,460
For a while, stores near gold mines would have scales at the register.

98
00:08:44,900 --> 00:08:47,640
A miner could pay for his purchases with a pinch of gold dust.

99
00:08:49,260 --> 00:08:51,540
The process was messy and inconsistent.

100
00:08:52,820 --> 00:08:55,180
A German metallurgist named Templeton Reed,

101
00:08:55,460 --> 00:08:58,840
who had come to America in search of gold, had a better idea.

102
00:09:00,420 --> 00:09:03,480
If the miners couldn't get their gold to the mint in Philadelphia,

103
00:09:03,481 --> 00:09:06,720
and he would build his own mint near the mines.

104
00:09:07,660 --> 00:09:08,620
An illegal act.

105
00:09:11,440 --> 00:09:13,180
Reed's mint was an immediate success.

106
00:09:14,000 --> 00:09:16,920
Within a year, nearly 40 other privately owned mints

107
00:09:16,921 --> 00:09:18,580
had opened in the southeast.

108
00:09:21,000 --> 00:09:22,700
Gold coins poured into the economy.

109
00:09:24,180 --> 00:09:25,620
And though the government wasn't happy

110
00:09:25,621 --> 00:09:27,720
with privately minted coins in circulation,

111
00:09:28,320 --> 00:09:31,160
it didn't have the resources to stop them from being made.

112
00:09:31,161 --> 00:09:41,660
The result of the influx of all these miners and the gold led to a re-evaluation of our nation's coinage.

113
00:09:41,940 --> 00:09:46,780
It also led to expanded economic trade and development in the area.

114
00:09:48,180 --> 00:09:51,920
Stores and shops opened. Merchants did booming business.

115
00:09:52,700 --> 00:09:56,620
But the frenzy of the East Coast gold rush subsided within two years.

116
00:09:57,460 --> 00:10:01,680
The mines dried up, and the privately owned mints closed down.

117
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:06,740
For the next decade, the making of money would remain the same.

118
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:13,240
But by the middle part of the century, a series of events would forever change how money was made in America.

119
00:10:14,320 --> 00:10:18,700
The gold rush of 1849 was just around the next bend.

120
00:10:21,220 --> 00:10:24,700
The words mint and money come from the Latin word moneta.

121
00:10:25,540 --> 00:10:28,500
Moneta was the name of the temple of the Roman goddess Juno,

122
00:10:28,920 --> 00:10:31,280
the place where Romans first struck their coins.

123
00:10:36,480 --> 00:10:41,580
On January 24, 1848, a carpenter named James Marshall

124
00:10:41,581 --> 00:10:45,260
was inspecting the site of a proposed lumber mill on the American River

125
00:10:45,261 --> 00:10:47,060
near what is today Sacramento.

126
00:10:50,720 --> 00:10:53,820
Marshall stumbled across some gold-colored nuggets.

127
00:10:54,700 --> 00:10:57,700
The great gold rush was on.

128
00:10:57,701 --> 00:11:00,700
Miners came in droves to Northern California.

129
00:11:00,701 --> 00:11:03,700
Gold flowed out of the hills.

130
00:11:03,701 --> 00:11:08,700
As a result of the gold rush, some $600 million worth of gold dust

131
00:11:08,701 --> 00:11:12,700
over a period of several years actually hit our nation's coffer,

132
00:11:12,701 --> 00:11:17,700
creating a huge economic impact not only on California,

133
00:11:17,701 --> 00:11:20,700
both politically, socially, and economically,

134
00:11:20,701 --> 00:11:23,700
but in the entire United States.

135
00:11:24,700 --> 00:11:30,082
Millionaires were created overnight. Construction was at an all-time high.

136
00:11:30,083 --> 00:11:32,700
Banks were flush with money to lend.

137
00:11:34,700 --> 00:11:39,700
But as in the East, there was concern over what to do with all the gold.

138
00:11:39,701 --> 00:11:42,700
Shipping the gold to the Philadelphia Mint was difficult.

139
00:11:42,701 --> 00:11:48,700
It would take six months before gold coins would make it back, if they came back at all.

140
00:11:48,701 --> 00:11:55,700
As a result, some miners started making deals with Mormon settlers.

141
00:11:55,701 --> 00:12:02,700
A few years before the discovery of gold at Sutter's Mill, the Mormons had discovered small pockets of gold in the Sierra Nevada.

142
00:12:02,701 --> 00:12:09,700
The Mormons had set up a small mint in Salt Lake City to strike their gold into coins.

143
00:12:09,701 --> 00:12:16,700
But their mint couldn't handle the wagonloads of gold coming from the mountains east of San Francisco.

144
00:12:16,701 --> 00:12:24,700
The few $10 gold pieces produced by the Mormons during this period are today considered rare collector's items.

145
00:12:24,701 --> 00:12:30,860
Well, this sounds great, but it created a problem, especially in San Francisco.

146
00:12:31,020 --> 00:12:36,840
You would have sailors coming into town and abandoning their ships and going to the gold fields.

147
00:12:36,960 --> 00:12:42,420
You had farmers leaving their farms and going to the gold fields.

148
00:12:42,421 --> 00:12:46,220
The prices of necessities skyrocketed as a result.

149
00:12:46,680 --> 00:12:52,540
Rents tripled in a year and no coins.

150
00:12:54,700 --> 00:12:58,700
Miners tried to buy goods and services with the gold dust they carried with them.

151
00:12:59,780 --> 00:13:03,580
But as in the Carolinas, it was an inefficient way of doing business.

152
00:13:04,480 --> 00:13:09,520
You can imagine coming in from mining the gold

153
00:13:09,521 --> 00:13:12,520
and coming to San Francisco and entering a saloon,

154
00:13:13,040 --> 00:13:15,420
and in exchange for a shot of whiskey,

155
00:13:16,280 --> 00:13:20,880
the bartender would dip down with his thumb and forefingers

156
00:13:20,881 --> 00:13:24,340
and pinch out a bit of gold dust from the pouch.

157
00:13:25,640 --> 00:13:29,360
Well, pretty soon all the owners of the bars,

158
00:13:29,361 --> 00:13:30,780
and there were quite a few of them,

159
00:13:31,020 --> 00:13:33,100
would hire only big, burly guys.

160
00:13:33,640 --> 00:13:35,580
And they would ask him in their interview,

161
00:13:35,720 --> 00:13:37,600
now how much can you raise in a pinch?

162
00:13:39,520 --> 00:13:45,280
The business community in California pleaded with the federal government to establish a mint out west.

163
00:13:46,460 --> 00:13:50,400
The tons of gold coming into San Francisco was causing mass confusion.

164
00:13:52,860 --> 00:14:01,760
And while the gold rush boomed in California, other states were lobbying the federal government to open assay offices, or mints, in their jurisdictions.

165
00:14:01,761 --> 00:14:09,960
Pennsylvania, Georgia, North Carolina, and New York all wanted their own mint.

166
00:14:10,680 --> 00:14:14,400
Having a mint nearby guaranteed a constant flow of coins into the economy.

167
00:14:15,620 --> 00:14:22,000
And even though California's gold was more than enough to warrant a mint being built in the Golden State, it didn't happen.

168
00:14:24,460 --> 00:14:29,340
So, as in the East a decade earlier, private mints slowly began to fill the void.

169
00:14:29,341 --> 00:14:32,020
even though federal law prohibited them.

170
00:14:33,560 --> 00:14:38,840
The first coins privately issued in California

171
00:14:38,841 --> 00:14:45,220
was in May of 1849 by Norris Greig and Norris out of New York.

172
00:14:45,221 --> 00:14:49,800
And they were located in Benicia, which is north of San Francisco.

173
00:14:50,340 --> 00:14:53,600
They issued $5 gold pieces before they moved to Stockton.

174
00:14:53,920 --> 00:14:57,120
We know they moved to Stockton because one coin has shown up

175
00:14:57,121 --> 00:14:59,160
with the word Stockton on it.

176
00:14:59,340 --> 00:15:04,540
It's the only one we know of, so if you find another one, it'll be worth a lot of money.

177
00:15:06,740 --> 00:15:11,020
The first coin makers in California actually didn't make many coins at all.

178
00:15:11,740 --> 00:15:17,340
They instead made gold ingots, because they weren't sure what penalties they would incur for striking coins.

179
00:15:18,860 --> 00:15:22,580
In 1851, after two years of gold mining in California,

180
00:15:23,280 --> 00:15:26,400
the federal government finally decided it needed to do something.

181
00:15:26,401 --> 00:15:32,500
The U.S. assayer of gold sent a man named Augustus Humbert to San Francisco.

182
00:15:33,560 --> 00:15:38,329
He brought dyes with him and planned to contract with a local gold coin company to make U.S.

183
00:15:38,330 --> 00:15:39,030
coins.

184
00:15:40,120 --> 00:15:47,520
The government chose to work with James Moffat, who owned the private mint of Moffat & Company, because of the firm's solid reputation.

185
00:15:47,521 --> 00:15:56,920
upon hearing that u.s coins were going to be minted in california most coin makers began

186
00:15:56,921 --> 00:16:01,620
melting down their gold coins in anticipation of selling the raw material to the government

187
00:16:01,621 --> 00:16:11,740
but there was a hitch the government only issued 50 gold coins with most of the smaller coins now

188
00:16:11,741 --> 00:16:15,940
destroyed. There was no way for citizens to make change.

189
00:16:15,980 --> 00:16:21,020
So you had to turn this dust into something that was acceptable.

190
00:16:21,040 --> 00:16:24,380
And really, the only coins they liked were by Augustus Humbert,

191
00:16:24,420 --> 00:16:26,820
and they were $50 gold slugs.

192
00:16:26,860 --> 00:16:29,800
Well, try and change a $50 bill today when there's been a little

193
00:16:29,820 --> 00:16:34,160
inflation since 1848, so these things got unwieldy.

194
00:16:34,200 --> 00:16:38,800
Essentially, you had no one producing coins that were

195
00:16:38,840 --> 00:16:41,540
acceptable for commerce, and it's sort of hard to carry around

196
00:16:41,541 --> 00:16:42,580
bag of dust.

197
00:16:42,581 --> 00:16:49,080
In 1854, the federal government could no longer ignore what was going on in

198
00:16:49,081 --> 00:16:49,990
California.

199
00:16:49,991 --> 00:16:56,940
The San Francisco Mint opened, in the building used by Moffett and Company to strike

200
00:16:56,941 --> 00:17:03,069
private coins. But gold production in Northern California had begun to slow.

201
00:17:03,070 --> 00:17:04,440
A new metal would

202
00:17:04,441 --> 00:17:06,460
supplement the economy's need for coins.

203
00:17:08,140 --> 00:17:14,200
In 1859, when the Comstock Lode, the world's richest silver strike, was discovered near

204
00:17:14,201 --> 00:17:18,900
Virginia City, Nevada, the sleepy village turned into a bustling mining town.

205
00:17:21,720 --> 00:17:27,540
Excavations along the eastern slope of Mount Davidson yielded more than $300 million in

206
00:17:27,541 --> 00:17:29,280
silver over 20 years.

207
00:17:29,281 --> 00:17:36,160
With gold getting scarcer, silver became a major ingredient in the making of coins.

208
00:17:37,080 --> 00:17:41,700
Soon stagecoaches loaded with silver galloped from Nevada toward the San Francisco Mint.

209
00:17:44,660 --> 00:17:46,920
But success came at a high price.

210
00:17:47,800 --> 00:17:50,440
With so much money being transported through California,

211
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:55,520
it was only a matter of time before a different kind of gold rush gripped the state.

212
00:17:55,521 --> 00:18:05,220
For every honest miner trying to get his strike to a mint, there were two others who were willing to take it from him, by whatever means necessary.

213
00:18:07,400 --> 00:18:16,620
In San Francisco, the mint became a symbol of triumph, when in 1906, it still stood after a killer earthquake devastated the city.

214
00:18:16,621 --> 00:18:28,300
They're very famous photos of the old San Francisco Mint standing proudly among the

215
00:18:28,301 --> 00:18:33,087
rubble of the rest of San Francisco after the 1906 earthquake.

216
00:18:33,088 --> 00:18:34,940
And that building really

217
00:18:34,941 --> 00:18:41,700
was a symbol of the resilience of the city and its determination to return from that

218
00:18:41,701 --> 00:18:42,700
devastation.

219
00:18:42,701 --> 00:18:50,080
Though it had stopped mass-producing coins by the mid-1950s, the San Francisco Mint still

220
00:18:50,081 --> 00:18:53,660
struck commemorative coins for collectors.

221
00:18:53,661 --> 00:19:00,100
Sadly the building would have to be shut down after the 1989 Loma Prieta earthquake.

222
00:19:00,101 --> 00:19:07,560
The century-old facility could not withstand the shock of a second major quake.

223
00:19:07,561 --> 00:19:11,400
In 1863, officials at the U.S. Mint suggested the words,

224
00:19:11,700 --> 00:19:17,100
Our Country, Our God, or God Our Trust, be placed on all U.S. coins.

225
00:19:17,940 --> 00:19:22,680
The Secretary of the Treasury amended the suggestion to, In God We Trust.

226
00:19:26,860 --> 00:19:32,720
During the heady time of the mid-1800s, the making of money in the West was dirty business.

227
00:19:33,720 --> 00:19:38,240
Murder, deception, and greed became hallmarks of the dark side of the gold rush.

228
00:19:39,900 --> 00:19:44,640
Mining companies had to hire special guards to keep a close eye on their employees.

229
00:19:45,480 --> 00:19:50,320
The temptation of pure gold and silver in the pocket could lure even the most honorable of men.

230
00:19:51,140 --> 00:19:57,320
You had people who were taking these $50 gold pieces, which were two and a half ounces of gold,

231
00:19:57,321 --> 00:20:00,860
wrapping them perhaps in a handkerchief or whatever

232
00:20:00,861 --> 00:20:03,960
and slugging people over the head

233
00:20:03,961 --> 00:20:08,620
to relieve them of their gold coins or gold dust or whatever,

234
00:20:09,320 --> 00:20:13,620
and perhaps that's where we got the term $50 gold slug.

235
00:20:15,480 --> 00:20:17,940
Stagecoaches carrying gold and silver

236
00:20:17,941 --> 00:20:19,980
from the mines to the San Francisco Mint

237
00:20:19,981 --> 00:20:21,540
were constant targets.

238
00:20:23,380 --> 00:20:26,520
In 1864, in Carson City, Nevada,

239
00:20:26,521 --> 00:20:28,420
not far from the Comstock load,

240
00:20:29,040 --> 00:20:30,760
private miners started their own mint.

241
00:20:32,140 --> 00:20:34,840
It was safer to make the short trip to the Nevada capital

242
00:20:34,841 --> 00:20:37,840
and risk being robbed along the roads to San Francisco.

243
00:20:41,080 --> 00:20:43,840
It was eventually taken over by the federal government.

244
00:20:44,880 --> 00:20:46,400
It closed in 1893

245
00:20:46,401 --> 00:20:51,420
after striking more than $50 million worth of Carson City's silver dollars

246
00:20:51,421 --> 00:20:52,640
and other coins.

247
00:20:52,641 --> 00:20:58,240
While Carson City thrived on the Comstock Lode Silver,

248
00:20:59,040 --> 00:21:01,220
a third American gold rush began.

249
00:21:02,480 --> 00:21:06,360
In 1860, we had another gold rush,

250
00:21:06,600 --> 00:21:10,380
this time in Colorado, hikes, peak, or bust.

251
00:21:11,020 --> 00:21:14,580
And many of the people who were involved in the California gold rush

252
00:21:14,581 --> 00:21:19,280
went out to Colorado to get involved in mining and minting coins.

253
00:21:19,281 --> 00:21:23,460
Once again, there was a need for a new medium of exchange

254
00:21:23,461 --> 00:21:25,620
with the influx of people in the area.

255
00:21:26,520 --> 00:21:32,640
And a major banking house by the name of Clark Gruber and Company,

256
00:21:33,400 --> 00:21:37,320
who was buying up the gold dust at the time

257
00:21:37,321 --> 00:21:40,340
and shipping the gold dust out to Philadelphia

258
00:21:40,341 --> 00:21:43,280
and having it come back as coins,

259
00:21:43,820 --> 00:21:46,780
got the idea that, hey, we can do it right here.

260
00:21:46,781 --> 00:21:54,480
The mining company authorized an artist to design a coin signifying the gold discovered at Pikes Peak.

261
00:21:56,060 --> 00:22:02,140
Unfortunately, this gentleman never had made it out to Colorado to see what Pikes Peak looks like.

262
00:22:02,840 --> 00:22:07,540
So the first dyes that came out were of a mountain, to be sure,

263
00:22:07,740 --> 00:22:11,220
but not the one that people were familiar with in the area.

264
00:22:11,900 --> 00:22:17,780
So these 1860 $10 and $20 gold pieces that had this mountain on it

265
00:22:17,781 --> 00:22:21,920
were soon discredited, and people refused to take them,

266
00:22:22,020 --> 00:22:25,000
and as a result, they were forced to come up with new dyes

267
00:22:25,001 --> 00:22:29,720
with the familiar liberty head on one side and the eagle on the other.

268
00:22:30,540 --> 00:22:34,360
For the next 30 years, the debate over how to make the nation's money

269
00:22:34,361 --> 00:22:36,120
and how to standardize it continued.

270
00:22:36,740 --> 00:22:40,280
By the late 1890s, the federal government pushed for a gold standard

271
00:22:40,281 --> 00:22:43,120
in which every piece of currency would be backed by gold.

272
00:22:43,920 --> 00:22:47,660
Coins were made of precious metal, gold and silver,

273
00:22:48,100 --> 00:22:55,000
so that a $20 gold piece was said to contain $20 worth of precious metal.

274
00:22:56,420 --> 00:23:00,520
In 1896, presidential candidate William Jennings Bryan,

275
00:23:00,940 --> 00:23:04,360
a bimetallist, delivered his famous cross of gold speech.

276
00:23:04,361 --> 00:23:11,020
Brian wanted U.S. coins to continue to be backed by both gold and silver.

277
00:23:12,340 --> 00:23:15,060
Known for being one of the nation's most outspoken orators,

278
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:18,660
Brian blasted those who wanted a gold standard saying,

279
00:23:19,660 --> 00:23:23,700
You shall not press down upon the born of labor, this crown of thorns.

280
00:23:24,400 --> 00:23:28,560
You shall not crucify mankind upon a cross of gold.

281
00:23:31,480 --> 00:23:33,380
Though his speech may have been dramatic,

282
00:23:33,381 --> 00:23:38,220
Ryan lost the election, and the Gold Standard Act was passed in 1900.

283
00:23:40,580 --> 00:23:45,200
With all forms of money issued or coined by the United States now backed by gold,

284
00:23:45,640 --> 00:23:48,480
the government had to control the production of money carefully.

285
00:23:50,100 --> 00:23:52,040
Throughout the first half of the 20th century,

286
00:23:52,860 --> 00:23:55,640
coin production in the United States remained about the same,

287
00:23:56,700 --> 00:24:00,440
between 200 million and 500 million coins each year.

288
00:24:00,441 --> 00:24:11,300
But during the Depression years of 1931 through 1933, the mint struck a total of just 70 million coins for all three years.

289
00:24:14,180 --> 00:24:20,200
Less than a decade later, during World War II, the mint struck up to 2 billion coins a year.

290
00:24:22,360 --> 00:24:25,191
During the last part of the 19th century, the U.S.

291
00:24:25,192 --> 00:24:27,400
mint began to make commemorative coins.

292
00:24:28,400 --> 00:24:36,260
Historical events, such as the Battle of Gettysburg, were placed on coins as a way of enshrining some of the most important episodes in American history.

293
00:24:37,340 --> 00:24:38,960
Even presidents got into the act.

294
00:24:39,580 --> 00:24:44,400
Teddy Roosevelt worked closely with a famous artist to create one of the nation's most enduring coins.

295
00:24:44,860 --> 00:24:51,160
Teddy Roosevelt was a collector, and he also took an interest in art.

296
00:24:51,161 --> 00:24:58,038
And he worked hand in glove, really, with the great American artist engraver St.

297
00:24:58,039 --> 00:24:58,739
Gaudens

298
00:24:58,740 --> 00:25:04,360
in the creation of what I think is one of the most beautiful coins in the world

299
00:25:04,361 --> 00:25:07,920
and certainly one of the most beautiful coins produced by this country,

300
00:25:08,500 --> 00:25:10,800
which is the American Eagle design.

301
00:25:11,220 --> 00:25:17,580
It's the standing liberty that graces the gold coin of the United States even today.

302
00:25:17,581 --> 00:25:21,046
Sometimes coins such as the Susan B.

303
00:25:21,047 --> 00:25:25,860
Anthony were put into circulation without success.

304
00:25:25,861 --> 00:25:28,474
The dollar piece had a major flaw.

305
00:25:28,475 --> 00:25:33,240
It looked and felt just like a quarter and quickly fell out of

306
00:25:33,241 --> 00:25:39,623
favor. It's remarkable how deeply held people's feelings are about coinage.

307
00:25:39,624 --> 00:25:41,580
In currency in general,

308
00:25:41,581 --> 00:25:46,102
what I've really found is that there's a lot of resistance to making changes.

309
00:25:46,103 --> 00:25:47,100
And when you make

310
00:25:47,101 --> 00:25:52,100
changes you really have to explain why you need to make them.

311
00:25:52,101 --> 00:25:54,122
However, in 1999, the U.S.

312
00:25:54,123 --> 00:25:59,100
Mint implemented the 50 State Quarters Program, which introduces

313
00:25:59,101 --> 00:26:03,200
five new quarters every year for ten years.

314
00:26:03,201 --> 00:26:07,320
Each one bears a reverse design honoring one of the 50 states.

315
00:26:07,321 --> 00:26:11,700
The coins are released in the order that the states join the Union.

316
00:26:11,701 --> 00:26:17,080
Besides protecting bullion depositories at Fort Knox, Kentucky, the Mint Overseas Depository

317
00:26:17,081 --> 00:26:21,440
at West Point, New York, and has coinage facilities in Philadelphia and Denver.

318
00:26:23,280 --> 00:26:26,760
The act of striking coins is the same as it was 100 years ago.

319
00:26:27,140 --> 00:26:28,440
It's just done much faster.

320
00:26:30,420 --> 00:26:33,960
But what has changed is the material with which coins are made.

321
00:26:34,680 --> 00:26:40,640
The Coinage Act of 1965 eliminated all silver from the nation's dimes and quarters.

322
00:26:40,641 --> 00:26:49,260
In the United States, we shifted from a precious metal or silver base coinage in the 1960s.

323
00:26:49,940 --> 00:26:58,960
And by the late 1960s, all of our coin denominations had moved from a silver base to a base metal,

324
00:26:59,640 --> 00:27:03,840
in most cases a copper-nickel or copper-nickel combination.

325
00:27:03,841 --> 00:27:09,480
If you pick up a quarter that is dated in the mid-1960s or earlier,

326
00:27:09,680 --> 00:27:12,880
if you can find one of those and you can look at it from the side,

327
00:27:13,360 --> 00:27:19,020
it's all one color, and that's because it's a 90% silver coin.

328
00:27:19,700 --> 00:27:23,040
If you look at the more modern versions of the quarter, they look like a sandwich.

329
00:27:23,420 --> 00:27:27,140
You have the red inner layer and the silver outer layers,

330
00:27:27,260 --> 00:27:30,500
and that's that copper-nickel sandwich that we use today.

331
00:27:30,501 --> 00:27:37,500
While coins may have had an eventful history, paper money's story is equally dramatic,

332
00:27:37,501 --> 00:27:43,500
beginning with its becoming the government's most powerful weapon in wartime.

333
00:27:43,501 --> 00:27:44,609
Whenever the U.S.

334
00:27:44,610 --> 00:27:49,500
Mint has debated discontinuing the penny, the idea has been met with public

335
00:27:49,501 --> 00:27:56,500
outrage, especially in Illinois, the home state of Abraham Lincoln.

336
00:27:56,501 --> 00:28:04,120
At the start of the Civil War, both the North and the South quickly realized they needed one thing very badly.

337
00:28:05,160 --> 00:28:05,860
Money.

338
00:28:06,480 --> 00:28:07,180
U.S.

339
00:28:07,181 --> 00:28:13,580
President Abraham Lincoln and Confederate President Jefferson Davis did the only thing they could do to raise more capital.

340
00:28:14,140 --> 00:28:14,880
They printed money.

341
00:28:16,360 --> 00:28:21,160
Paper currency was initially issued during the Civil War to fund the Civil War.

342
00:28:21,640 --> 00:28:27,020
The need for money far exceeded the coinage available at that time.

343
00:28:27,460 --> 00:28:33,660
The simplest, most expeditious way of getting money into the system at that time

344
00:28:33,661 --> 00:28:35,400
was to print paper notes.

345
00:28:36,880 --> 00:28:40,000
Though private institutions such as banks and businesses

346
00:28:40,001 --> 00:28:42,860
had printed their own paper money for more than 50 years,

347
00:28:43,400 --> 00:28:45,860
this was the first time for the federal government.

348
00:28:45,861 --> 00:28:49,188
Many experts believe that the first U.S.

349
00:28:49,189 --> 00:28:54,680
dollars were printed with green ink out of fear of counterfeiting.

350
00:28:56,200 --> 00:29:01,540
The same technology that allowed photographer Matthew Brady to record the images of the Civil War

351
00:29:01,541 --> 00:29:04,420
could have been used to make duplicates of currency.

352
00:29:06,740 --> 00:29:09,780
Green ink was applied to one side of the nation's new currency

353
00:29:09,781 --> 00:29:13,060
because it could not be duplicated with black and white photography.

354
00:29:13,061 --> 00:29:17,060
green ink was more chemically stable than other colors,

355
00:29:17,061 --> 00:29:21,060
and engraving officials believed the color green represented stability.

356
00:29:21,061 --> 00:29:27,060
Many experts believe that is where the term greenback first originated.

357
00:29:27,061 --> 00:29:28,804
And though the first U.S.

358
00:29:28,805 --> 00:29:33,060
dollars were slightly larger than those in circulation today,

359
00:29:33,061 --> 00:29:38,060
the ink and the paper on which our money is printed is virtually the same.

360
00:29:38,061 --> 00:29:44,233
the same. It's made of cotton and linen fibers as opposed to wood pulp fibers.

361
00:29:44,234 --> 00:29:45,420
It's not woven,

362
00:29:45,560 --> 00:29:51,400
it's not a cloth, it is a paper, but the material is a little different than your traditional paper.

363
00:29:52,180 --> 00:29:59,660
And this rag paper was so called in the old days because the cotton and linen fibers were actually

364
00:29:59,661 --> 00:30:01,160
obtained from rags.

365
00:30:01,161 --> 00:30:07,160
Because the new paper money was backed by nothing more than the government's

366
00:30:07,161 --> 00:30:08,147
willingness to pay.

367
00:30:08,148 --> 00:30:11,680
Its value depended on the fortunes of the armies on the battlefield.

368
00:30:13,560 --> 00:30:19,860
When the Confederate government was doing well, its notes were high. And of course,

369
00:30:19,980 --> 00:30:24,138
as it started losing the war, they depreciated rapidly.

370
00:30:24,139 --> 00:30:26,860
The Union notes were the same thing.

371
00:30:27,940 --> 00:30:34,623
Beginning in 62, they almost circulated at par, and then they started dropping.

372
00:30:34,624 --> 00:30:35,324
In 1864,

373
00:30:35,325 --> 00:30:39,880
or they got down to 38 cents on the gold dollar.

374
00:30:41,960 --> 00:30:43,900
Merchants were vulnerable to swindlers.

375
00:30:45,180 --> 00:30:48,100
Cheats would try to pass devalued paper money off at par,

376
00:30:48,300 --> 00:30:51,020
its face value, even though they knew it was worthless.

377
00:30:53,680 --> 00:30:57,640
A lot of times the merchants would put an ad in the newspapers

378
00:30:57,641 --> 00:31:01,580
saying, so-and-so likes to drink his lager beer,

379
00:31:01,581 --> 00:31:04,160
but he doesn't like to pay for it.

380
00:31:04,161 --> 00:31:08,480
You know, today he offered me at par for this bill,

381
00:31:08,760 --> 00:31:12,800
and of course I had to accept it or take nothing, be warned of this guy.

382
00:31:14,020 --> 00:31:17,500
But there was one place in the union that paper money was not welcome.

383
00:31:18,480 --> 00:31:22,080
The far western states, especially California.

384
00:31:23,640 --> 00:31:25,260
Californians hated paper currency.

385
00:31:26,340 --> 00:31:30,440
The state passed laws to ensure that paper notes would not circulate in its economy.

386
00:31:30,441 --> 00:31:33,900
It was easy for California to do.

387
00:31:34,580 --> 00:31:37,100
The state was still flush with coins from the gold rush.

388
00:31:37,640 --> 00:31:40,680
The assistant treasurer of the United States

389
00:31:40,681 --> 00:31:43,820
came and spoke on the glories of greenbacks.

390
00:31:44,920 --> 00:31:47,040
And, of course, the audience was very appreciative.

391
00:31:47,340 --> 00:31:50,060
They yelled, hang him, hang him, hang him,

392
00:31:50,420 --> 00:31:51,680
chased him out of the building,

393
00:31:52,100 --> 00:31:54,480
and they took refuge in a drugstore.

394
00:31:55,360 --> 00:31:57,160
But, you know, if they'd caught him,

395
00:31:57,161 --> 00:32:02,620
you might have had the top federal government financial official dangling from a line post.

396
00:32:04,040 --> 00:32:06,900
San Franciscans were especially hateful of paper money.

397
00:32:07,820 --> 00:32:13,920
As an affront to the government, people who lived in the Bay Area would gladly accept only one type of paper money,

398
00:32:14,780 --> 00:32:19,400
that issued by a local character who believed himself to be of royal descent.

399
00:32:20,820 --> 00:32:25,080
And that was put out by His Imperial Majesty Norton I,

400
00:32:25,081 --> 00:32:30,080
emperor of the United States and protector of Mexico.

401
00:32:30,081 --> 00:32:34,080
Poor Joshua Norton had come from South Africa,

402
00:32:34,081 --> 00:32:37,080
very successful Gold Rush Commission merchant,

403
00:32:37,081 --> 00:32:40,080
tried to corner the rice market,

404
00:32:40,081 --> 00:32:42,080
lost when ships came in,

405
00:32:42,081 --> 00:32:45,080
and also his bankers weren't very nice to him,

406
00:32:45,081 --> 00:32:47,080
and he lost part of his mind.

407
00:32:47,081 --> 00:32:50,080
Well, in the 70s, he started printing his own money,

408
00:32:50,081 --> 00:32:52,080
usually in 50-cent increments,

409
00:32:52,081 --> 00:32:56,840
And, of course, people would accept the imperial bonds

410
00:32:56,841 --> 00:32:59,820
to help out Joshua Norton.

411
00:33:00,560 --> 00:33:02,520
In fact, when he died in 1880,

412
00:33:02,620 --> 00:33:05,000
his funeral was the largest procession

413
00:33:05,001 --> 00:33:06,360
that San Francisco had ever seen.

414
00:33:08,120 --> 00:33:10,360
Eventually, Californians, too,

415
00:33:10,640 --> 00:33:12,780
began to accept government-backed paper money.

416
00:33:13,820 --> 00:33:15,760
In the decades after the Civil War,

417
00:33:16,280 --> 00:33:18,680
paper money became not just a means to do business,

418
00:33:19,040 --> 00:33:20,720
but an expression of national culture.

419
00:33:22,080 --> 00:33:28,620
In 1895, for example, world-renowned artists were commissioned to create elaborate designs

420
00:33:28,621 --> 00:33:33,500
on a new series of banknotes.

421
00:33:33,501 --> 00:33:38,060
It was called the Educational Series, and the bills featured scenes from the fields

422
00:33:38,061 --> 00:33:41,940
of arts and science.

423
00:33:41,941 --> 00:33:44,680
One bill showed a semi-nude woman.

424
00:33:44,681 --> 00:33:49,300
It so enraged the wives of bankers in New England that the Bureau of Engraving and Printing

425
00:33:49,301 --> 00:33:53,460
was forced to pull the bill from circulation.

426
00:33:53,461 --> 00:33:55,260
The incident is where the phrase,

427
00:33:55,261 --> 00:33:58,180
banned in Boston, originated.

428
00:33:58,181 --> 00:34:00,600
After decades of trying out new looks,

429
00:34:00,601 --> 00:34:04,040
the US Treasury chose a consistent design and size.

430
00:34:04,041 --> 00:34:05,980
In the 1920s, the decision was made

431
00:34:05,981 --> 00:34:08,400
to standardize the design of currency

432
00:34:08,401 --> 00:34:11,860
and to reduce the size to the current 6 1⁄4 inches

433
00:34:11,861 --> 00:34:13,640
by 2 1⁄4 inches.

434
00:34:13,641 --> 00:34:15,480
And that change in size was actually

435
00:34:15,481 --> 00:34:18,135
done as a production enhancement.

436
00:34:18,136 --> 00:34:22,480
Obviously, printing a smaller note provides more notes

437
00:34:23,100 --> 00:34:29,500
per every turn of the press, so that it was an economic move to be able to produce more

438
00:34:29,501 --> 00:34:33,580
currency at a lower cost per note.

439
00:34:33,581 --> 00:34:40,140
Although Americans soon became used to their uniform dimes and nickels, ones and one-hundreds,

440
00:34:40,141 --> 00:34:44,600
and the government became used to making money efficiently, that didn't mean it could make

441
00:34:44,601 --> 00:34:46,124
endless amounts of it.

442
00:34:46,125 --> 00:34:50,420
A few years earlier, Woodrow Wilson signed the Federal Reserve

443
00:34:50,421 --> 00:34:55,198
Act in order to stabilize the nation's money and banking system.

444
00:34:55,199 --> 00:34:56,840
It provided an elastic

445
00:34:56,841 --> 00:35:05,840
currency that could expand and contract in response to the economy's changing demands.

446
00:35:05,841 --> 00:35:06,541
The U.S.

447
00:35:06,542 --> 00:35:11,320
Mint and the Bureau of Printing and Engraving operate as manufacturing plants.

448
00:35:11,321 --> 00:35:16,840
Their only customer is the Federal Reserve Bank, which has 12 districts nationwide that

449
00:35:16,841 --> 00:35:21,120
monitor the economy's money supply.

450
00:35:21,121 --> 00:35:27,080
When the amount of available cash in a district falls short of acceptable levels, the Federal

451
00:35:27,081 --> 00:35:31,620
Reserve places an order with the Mint and the Bureau of Engraving and Printing to deliver

452
00:35:31,621 --> 00:35:37,160
more money.

453
00:35:37,161 --> 00:35:41,160
Federal Reserve notes are the only U.S. currency issued today.

454
00:35:41,161 --> 00:35:44,160
Though the notes look nearly the same as they did a century ago,

455
00:35:44,161 --> 00:35:49,160
recently added security features have made our hard-earned cash smarter

456
00:35:49,161 --> 00:35:53,160
and safer against counterfeiters.

457
00:35:53,161 --> 00:35:53,861
The U.S.

458
00:35:53,862 --> 00:35:58,160
Mint and Bureau of Engraving and Printing have also made money for foreign governments,

459
00:35:58,161 --> 00:36:03,160
including Mexico, the Philippines, and Cuba.

460
00:36:03,161 --> 00:36:08,402
There are approximately $650 billion worth of U.S.

461
00:36:08,403 --> 00:36:12,700
currency in circulation around the world.

462
00:36:13,220 --> 00:36:18,480
The Federal Reserve estimates about 60% of that actually circulates outside of the United States.

463
00:36:18,481 --> 00:36:21,520
U.S. currency is considered the world's currency.

464
00:36:21,940 --> 00:36:27,660
It is used in many countries as their day-to-day currency, and others people hold it as a store of value.

465
00:36:28,820 --> 00:36:32,720
And because it is the world's currency, it must be highly guarded.

466
00:36:33,160 --> 00:36:33,860
The U.S.

467
00:36:33,861 --> 00:36:40,160
Secret Service works in conjunction with the Treasury Department to prevent counterfeiting.

468
00:36:43,160 --> 00:36:46,160
With advanced digital technology, their job is increasingly difficult.

469
00:36:46,161 --> 00:36:52,160
Counterfeiting over the last decade or so has tended to increase due to the availability

470
00:36:52,161 --> 00:36:53,708
of reprographic equipment.

471
00:36:53,709 --> 00:36:57,160
Color copiers, inkjet printers, computer scanners have put

472
00:36:57,161 --> 00:37:04,840
have put the capability to reproduce a semblance of a currency note in the hands of most of us.

473
00:37:06,160 --> 00:37:12,800
Therefore, the Treasury Department continues to design new bills with advanced security features to foil counterfeiters.

474
00:37:13,500 --> 00:37:18,520
In 2003, the most used and counterfeited note in the U.S., the $20 bill,

475
00:37:19,000 --> 00:37:22,220
became the first in a series of new color money to be released.

476
00:37:22,221 --> 00:37:33,980
For any bill, the way to defeat tech-savvy counterfeiters starts with an ancient art.

477
00:37:33,981 --> 00:37:38,980
Inside a cramped office in the Bureau of Engraving and Printing in Washington, D.C., a handful

478
00:37:38,981 --> 00:37:47,060
of skilled artists design the metal dies that will print U.S. currency.

479
00:37:47,061 --> 00:37:51,260
There are only seven engravers in the United States who do this work.

480
00:37:51,261 --> 00:37:57,020
Each one has a specific job, concentrating on individual aspects of the bill, such as

481
00:37:57,021 --> 00:37:59,900
the portrait or the numbers.

482
00:37:59,901 --> 00:38:06,120
Doing a specific task gives each engraver a chance to become expert at his or her art.

483
00:38:06,121 --> 00:38:14,280
It also prevents one person from having all the skills needed to make bills on their own.

484
00:38:14,281 --> 00:38:20,040
The engravers work to create a master die, which is then reproduced onto plates and used

485
00:38:20,041 --> 00:38:27,040
for printing at facilities in Washington, D.C. and Fort Worth, Texas.

486
00:38:27,041 --> 00:38:32,860
Each piece of a bill, the portrait, the numbers, the dates and signature, are all done by different

487
00:38:32,861 --> 00:38:34,860
engravers.

488
00:38:34,861 --> 00:38:39,920
Tom Hipschen has been an engraver for the Bureau for 35 years.

489
00:38:39,921 --> 00:38:47,960
He engraved the portrait of Andrew Jackson on the new generation $20 bill.

490
00:38:47,961 --> 00:38:50,840
The art of engraving itself hasn't changed much.

491
00:38:50,841 --> 00:38:54,720
The tools are very similar to what they were 400 years ago.

492
00:38:54,721 --> 00:38:59,360
But the technical aspects of it have changed a great deal.

493
00:38:59,361 --> 00:39:05,920
We spend a lot of time checking the depths and widths of lines for printability, tailored

494
00:39:05,921 --> 00:39:10,560
just for the machines that we're going to use to reproduce it.

495
00:39:10,561 --> 00:39:17,900
To make the engraver's job even more complicated, all their work is done in reverse.

496
00:39:17,901 --> 00:39:22,340
Because it's a direct printing process, I actually have to engrave it in reverse in steel.

497
00:39:22,341 --> 00:39:26,300
So I make a very accurate tracing of the image and lay it on the surface of the steel in

498
00:39:26,301 --> 00:39:33,900
reverse and draw it very carefully the way I want it to appear using sharp steel points.

499
00:39:33,901 --> 00:39:38,580
We still need hand engravers to engrave like the portrait and the vignette on the back.

500
00:39:38,581 --> 00:39:47,320
Secret Service relies heavily on our hand engraving in their counterfeit deterrent detection.

501
00:39:47,321 --> 00:39:54,300
notes, you can tell a real difference between an engraved portrait and the photocopies.

502
00:39:55,100 --> 00:40:01,500
The new 20 also has subtle background colors, green, peach, and blue on both sides.

503
00:40:03,580 --> 00:40:09,000
Adding color provides the Bureau of Engraving and Printing the opportunity to add additional

504
00:40:09,001 --> 00:40:14,020
security features to make the note more complex, to make it harder for people to be able to

505
00:40:14,021 --> 00:40:19,640
accurately copy to reproduce, make it easier for people to recognize, keep people's interest

506
00:40:19,641 --> 00:40:23,100
up, and make our notes safer, smarter, more secure.

507
00:40:25,780 --> 00:40:29,760
Not since 1905 has any U.S. currency had a colored background.

508
00:40:30,660 --> 00:40:34,420
Then the $20 gold certificate had a golden tint and a red seal.

509
00:40:35,560 --> 00:40:38,660
Today's new color money costs about 8 cents per note to make.

510
00:40:39,280 --> 00:40:43,860
And for the first time, the Bureau of Engraving and Printing used computer technology in its

511
00:40:43,861 --> 00:40:44,561
design.

512
00:40:44,562 --> 00:40:50,340
It was my responsibility to take the design which was already in place and transfer

513
00:40:50,341 --> 00:40:57,720
into the security computer and create vector graphics and use the computer modules to create

514
00:40:57,721 --> 00:41:02,260
fine line details in the background which color was added to and that's how you get

515
00:41:02,261 --> 00:41:05,242
the colors in the background of the 20.

516
00:41:05,243 --> 00:41:08,760
This unit produces the color on the background

517
00:41:08,761 --> 00:41:14,023
of the press. It will print all the different tones on the back of the note.

518
00:41:14,024 --> 00:41:15,200
On the other side

519
00:41:15,201 --> 00:41:19,900
of the press, we'll be doing the same thing, simultaneously printing the face so that the

520
00:41:19,901 --> 00:41:24,580
sheet gets printed one time, both face and back, producing all the colors that you'll see on the

521
00:41:24,581 --> 00:41:29,758
currency. After the color is added, the sheets are printed three more times.

522
00:41:29,759 --> 00:41:31,120
First, a traditional

523
00:41:31,121 --> 00:41:36,420
green 20 is printed on the back of the note, and then the portrait on the front is printed in black

524
00:41:36,421 --> 00:41:37,121
Inc.

525
00:41:37,122 --> 00:41:42,900
Additionally, the copper to green color shifting ink is printed for extra security.

526
00:41:44,620 --> 00:41:46,866
Andrew Jackson has a makeover as well.

527
00:41:46,867 --> 00:41:49,940
Andrew Jackson, of course, is still in the new bill.

528
00:41:50,600 --> 00:41:54,651
What's been removed is the circle that went around the portrait.

529
00:41:54,652 --> 00:41:56,360
It gives the note more of a

530
00:41:56,361 --> 00:42:02,100
presidential look. Despite the new changes, many features will remain the same.

531
00:42:02,101 --> 00:42:02,801
U.S.

532
00:42:02,802 --> 00:42:07,760
currency has been known since it's been printed here at the Viewer of Engraving and

533
00:42:07,761 --> 00:42:10,640
Printing in 1860 as greenbacks.

534
00:42:10,641 --> 00:42:16,560
The color of the back, the intaglio ink, that engraved image on the back has been and will

535
00:42:16,561 --> 00:42:18,280
always be green.

536
00:42:18,281 --> 00:42:20,320
The new ones will still be green on the back.

537
00:42:20,321 --> 00:42:21,780
We'll add some color underneath it.

538
00:42:21,781 --> 00:42:26,500
But the primary color, it's still green, it's still a greenback.

539
00:42:26,501 --> 00:42:32,020
As in its previous design and as in its counterparts, the 100 and the 50, there is a polymer thread

540
00:42:32,021 --> 00:42:33,500
embedded vertically in the paper,

541
00:42:33,920 --> 00:42:36,080
and has printed on it the bill's denomination.

542
00:42:37,860 --> 00:42:39,820
The watermark is also on the note,

543
00:42:40,160 --> 00:42:41,960
a faint image similar to the portrait,

544
00:42:42,500 --> 00:42:44,820
visible from both sides when held up to the light.

545
00:42:46,240 --> 00:42:48,120
The Bureau of Engraving and Printing

546
00:42:48,121 --> 00:42:51,200
will produce about 2 billion new $20 notes a year.

547
00:42:52,700 --> 00:42:54,280
Following the introduction of the new 20,

548
00:42:54,760 --> 00:42:57,560
there will be a new color 50 and 100.

549
00:42:58,080 --> 00:43:01,180
The colors on the upcoming 50 and 100 will be different.

550
00:43:01,181 --> 00:43:06,180
Part of the thought process was denomination by color.

551
00:43:06,181 --> 00:43:09,180
I know when the last change occurred,

552
00:43:09,181 --> 00:43:13,180
a lot of people complained that they looked too much alike.

553
00:43:13,181 --> 00:43:17,180
People were getting 20s mixed up with 50s by not looking at the portrait.

554
00:43:17,181 --> 00:43:23,180
The Next Gen series, each denomination will have a separate color combination.

555
00:43:24,180 --> 00:43:28,180
Right now we've made no decision on a 5 or a 10.

556
00:43:28,181 --> 00:43:31,980
However, those are under study and well could be changed in the future.

557
00:43:31,981 --> 00:43:35,780
As the look of our money changes, so does the way we spend it.

558
00:43:35,781 --> 00:43:38,780
Today's monetary system is rapidly evolving.

559
00:43:38,781 --> 00:43:44,080
Credit cards, ATM machines and electronic banking have become a way of life.

560
00:43:44,081 --> 00:43:49,280
As technology improves, there may come a day when the nation sees the death of money.

561
00:43:49,281 --> 00:43:56,680
But for now, those in charge of making money believe there will always be a need for cold, hard cash.

562
00:43:56,681 --> 00:43:58,434
Every piece of money the U.S.

563
00:43:58,436 --> 00:44:06,360
Mint and the Bureau of Engraving and Printing make is not only currency, but captures a moment in time and defines each generation.

564
00:44:07,480 --> 00:44:14,340
No other form of art carries with it the power and history that exists on every piece of legal tender.

